    45	## Problem
    46	
    47	`mix kogen.build <slug>` runs the whole Build in the invoking checkout. The
    48	Developer edits it, the controller verifies it, the Reviewer reads it and
    49	publication commits in it. So:
    50	
    51	- **Shaping stops during Builds.** The guard snapshots ignored files in that
    52	  checkout. A Shaping session that saves a Draft or approves a package there
    53	  fails the running Build (`l5Ur5WAv`, and three Builds of this Intent on
    54	  2026-09-22). Today the driver must not write in the checkout during a Build.
    55	- **Parallel Builds (order 8) are impossible**: one working tree, one index,
    56	  one set of files per checkout.
    57	- **Roles can write anywhere the user can.** The Developer runs with
    58	  `--dangerously-skip-permissions` (Claude Code) or
    59	  `--dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox` (Codex). Any role, helper or command it starts
    60	  can write the control checkout, its Git refs, other Candidates, login scopes or the user's
    61	  files. Only the prompt contract and after-the-fact checks stand in the way.
    62	- **Every role shares one harness home.** Every Claude Code session of this
    63	  repository (Shaping, Developer, Reviewer, every Build) writes into the one
    64	  login scope directory. A Build can't be given a home of its own the obvious
    65	  way: a private `HOME` hides the login keychain (probe case B), and a fresh
    66	  `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` has no login (case C). Separately, an inherited
    67	  `CLAUDE_SECURESTORAGE_CONFIG_DIR=""` makes a Kogen launch authenticate with the
    68	  user's **personal** Claude Code login (case F), and Kogen doesn't strip it.
    69	
    70	## Outcome
    71	
    72	Still one Build at a time: the global `.kogen/build.lock` stays in control.
    73	Order 8 replaces it.
    74	
    75	1. **Admission creates the Candidate.** From the clean, attached control
    76	   checkout at commit `A` on branch `B`, Build creates one linked worktree in
    77	   Kogen's own directory, outside the repository:
    78	   `<workspaces-root>/<project-id>/<slug>-<build-id>/`, on a new branch
    79	   `kogen/<slug>/<build-id>` at `A`.
    80	   - `<workspaces-root>` is `~/Library/Application Support/Kogen/build-workspaces`,
    81	     or `KOGEN_WORKSPACES_ROOT` if set. Tests always set it.
    82	   - `<project-id>` is the SHA-256 of the expanded control path, the same id
    83	     the login selectors use (`Kogen.ClaudeCode.project_id/1`).
    84	   - `<build-id>` is the scenario-tracking record id.
    85	
    86	   An existing path, branch or owner record with that name is refused, never
    87	   reused. Before the worktree, Kogen writes an owner record at
    88	   `<workspaces-root>/<project-id>/candidates/<build-id>.json` with these fields:
    89	   schema version, build id, Intent id, slug, title, control root, worktree path,
    90	   branch, admitted branch and commit, harness home, credential bindings, start
    91	   time, status (`running`, `stopped: <category>`,
    92	   `accepted-unpublished: <reason>`) and, when one exists, the Candidate commit.
    93	   The status is updated at every exit. A `running` status counts only while
    94	   this project's build lock is held by a live process. Otherwise it's reported
    95	   as `stopped: interrupted`.
    96	
    97	   The Candidate gets a plain recursive copy of control `deps/`. If `deps/` is
    98	   missing, admission stops before any launch and names `mix deps.get`; there is
    99	   no network fallback. It gets no `_build/` and compiles cold. The controller's
   100	   frozen Approved package bytes go to the Candidate's ignored
   101	   `.kogen/intents/approved/<slug>/`. Nothing else from control's ignored
   102	   state is copied: no `.kogen/build.lock`, `.kogen/runtime/`, `.kogen/codex/`
   103	   or `.codex/sessions/` (build-failure lesson 13). Creation is one function in one module
   104	   (`Kogen.Build.Workspace.create/…`), so a later seeding step lands in one
   105	   place.
   106	2. **Harness home per Build.** Admission also creates
   107	   `<workspaces-root>/<project-id>/harness/<build-id>/`, outside both the
   108	   Candidate and control, and uses it for every role launch of the Build:
   109	   - **Claude Code roles** get `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR=<harness home>/claude`. The
   110	     login stays where it is: `CLAUDE_SECURESTORAGE_CONFIG_DIR` is set to the
   111	     login scope path, and `HOME` stays the user's. Sessions, `.claude.json`
   112	     and other Claude Code state for the Build land in the harness home. The
   113	     Keychain item and its refresh lock are the scope's, shared by reference
   114	     with Shaping sessions.
   115	   - **Codex roles** get their operation root (private `HOME`, `XDG_*` and
   116	     sqlite) inside `<harness home>/codex`. `CODEX_HOME` stays the scope,
   117	     because Codex keeps `auth.json` and rollouts there and has no separate
   118	     auth-home variable.
   119	   - **Cross-harness Expert.** On a hybrid route the Developer reaches the
   120	     Expert through `mix kogen.expert`, which it runs from the Candidate. The
   121	     frozen Expert assignment (`Kogen.Harness.expert_environment/2`,
   122	     `KOGEN_EXPERT`) also carries the control root, the Build's binding for
   123	     the Expert's harness and the harness home, and the task uses them: cwd
   124	     the Candidate, the bound scope, operation root or config dir under the
   125	     harness home.
   126	   - The harness home survives publication as session evidence. It is removed
   127	     only together with a retained Candidate by `mix kogen.candidates.remove`.
   128	     Pruning is parked with Candidate prune.
   129	3. **Credentials per Build: bound by reference, never copied.** At admission
   130	   the controller resolves each role harness's login scope once, from the
   131	   **control** root (never the Candidate path), and checks readiness with the
   132	   Build's own launch environment (`claude auth status` with the per-Build
   133	   config dir, and the Codex login check). It records each binding (harness,
   134	   scope name, scope path, runtime version) in the owner record and in the
   135	   tracking record. Every launch of that Build uses exactly these bindings,
   136	   even if the Shaper changes the login selector during the Build.
   137	   - Kogen never reads, copies, links, moves or renames a credential or a scope
   138	     directory. It never sets a per-launch `HOME` for Claude Code.
   139	   - Every Kogen Claude Code launch (Build roles, Shape, login and status)
   140	     removes an inherited `CLAUDE_SECURESTORAGE_CONFIG_DIR` and sets its own.
   141	     Claude launches also remove the Codex adapter's credential prefixes
   142	     (`CODEX_`, `OPENAI_`, `AZURE_`, `CHATGPT_`), mirroring Codex's removal of
   143	     `ANTHROPIC_`.
   144	     Scope-native launches set it equal to `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`, which selects
   145	     the same Keychain item as today.
   146	4. **Routing.** Every fresh Developer turn, exact-session resume, native
   147	   helper, Jev packet, controller verification run, proof-selector run and
   148	   fresh Reviewer runs with the Candidate as its process cwd and Git toplevel.
   149	   - Candidate-side work gets the Candidate root explicitly: Candidate id,
   150	     changed paths, guarded-path capture, proof-selector existence and runs, the
   151	     controller-built report and review packet, citation snapshots, staging and
   152	     commit.
   153	   - Control-side items get the control root explicitly: build lock, tracking
   154	     record, verification context, state, history and receipts, runtime logs,
   155	     prompts and engine assets, route and config, and login-scope identity.
   156	   - `Harness.open` and the adapters' `open` take the control project
   157	     explicitly and a separate launch root (the Candidate). Their
   158	     `File.cwd!()` defaults are removed, not substituted.
   159	   - Codex trusts the Candidate as its project root. Its login scope still
   160	     keys on control.
   161	   - No `File.cwd!()` or re-resolved toplevel stands in for either root.
   162	5. **The controller judges the Candidate; hooks don't.** Intent #4's
   163	   controller runs `make check` and the selected targets with cwd equal to the
   164	   Candidate (`make -C <Candidate>`), as child processes outside every role's
   165	   process tree. Its receipts bind to the Candidate id. Editing the
   166	   Candidate's copies of `.codex/hooks/**`, `Makefile` recipes or
   167	   `scripts/check/**` can't change who judges, which is always the parent
   168	   controller loaded from control when the Build started.
   169	   - This Intent changes no hook script, hook registration or Claude settings
   170	     file (self-hosting, D9).
   171	   - Build calls `VerificationPolicy.preflight(targets, candidate_root)` with
   172	     the Candidate root explicitly, because the Candidate's hook files are the
   173	     ones the PreToolUse gate guard actually runs. The default root argument is
   174	     not used on any Build path.
   175	   - A Candidate edit to a hook file is outside this and most Intents' guards,
   176	     so the guarded-path check stops such a Build before verification, as
   177	     today. The offline proof uses a fixture Intent that guards
   178	     `.codex/hooks/**` to show the edited copies still change no verdict. The PreToolUse gate
   179	     guard stays a courtesy guard against running gates by hand, not a judge;
   180	     README says so.
   181	6. **Rework stays in the same Candidate.** Verification retries, every outer
   182	   resumption and each fresh Review reuse the same Candidate, the same harness
   183	   home and the same Developer session. No attempt creates another worktree or
   184	   home.
   185	7. **Publication.** On acceptance the controller writes the Complete package
   186	   into the Candidate and commits there, with today's checks: per-path and
   187	   aggregate staged-size limits, `.kogen/runtime/` rejection,
   188	   assume-unchanged/skip-worktree refusal, trailers and ordinary commit hooks.
   189	   1. In control it then requires: still on `B`, `B` still at `A`, control
   190	      clean by today's `clean_worktree?` rule, and no blinding index flags.
   191	   2. It fast-forwards `B` to the Candidate commit and updates control's index
   192	      and working tree in the same step (`git merge --ff-only` in control).
   193	   3. It asserts control's HEAD, tree and clean status.
   194	   4. It removes control's ignored Approved copy of this slug, then removes the
   195	      Candidate worktree (`git worktree remove`, no `--force`), its branch
   196	      (`git branch -d`) and its owner record.
   197	
   198	   No merge commit, rebase or re-verification happens. If step 4's worktree
   199	   removal is refused after the fast-forward (for example an untracked file in
   200	   the Candidate), `B` stays published. The worktree, branch and owner record
   201	   are kept with status `published: cleanup refused: <reason>`, the record's
   202	   disposition is `published-retained`, and the Build exits zero with a warning
   203	   naming the path and `mix kogen.candidates.remove <build-id>`.
   204	8. **Refused publication.** The Build refuses to publish if `B` moved, if
   205	   control is dirty or on another branch, or if the fast-forward fails. Then:
   206	   - `B`, control's index and working tree, and control's Approved package stay
   207	     unchanged.
   208	   - The Candidate worktree, branch (holding the accepted commit) and harness
   209	     home are kept.
   210	   - The Build exits non-zero. The message names the reason, the admitted and
   211	     current commits, the worktree path, the branch, the Candidate commit, the
   212	     tracking record and `mix kogen.candidates.remove <build-id>
   213	     --discard-accepted`.
   214	9. **Failure retention.** On any other stop (verification or outer allowance
   215	   exhausted, Jev cannot-comply, integrity failure, provider failure, malformed
   216	   Review):
   217	   - The Candidate worktree, branch and harness home are kept as they are.
   218	   - The stop message names the slug, build id, worktree path and branch next
   219	     to the record path, plus `mix kogen.candidates.remove <build-id>`.
   220	   - Nothing adopts, reuses or deletes a retained Candidate. The next Build of
   221	     the same Intent gets a new one.
   222	   - Worktrees without a Kogen owner record are never touched. That includes
   223	     the old `.kogen/runtime/build-worktrees/checkouts/*`.
   224	10. **Shaping keeps working during a Build.** While a Build runs, a Shaping
   225	    session in control may write Drafts, edit other packages under
   226	    `.kogen/intents/`, and move another package into `approved/`. It may also
   227	    use the same Kogen login at the same time. None of this fails the Build,
   228	    because guarded-path capture, changed paths and Candidate id read only the
   229	    Candidate. The Build still publishes, since ignored files don't make
   230	    control dirty. The Build's own Approved package stays protected in the
   231	    Candidate by today's approved-mutation check. A tracked-file edit in
   232	    control still makes publication refuse (outcome 8) and keeps the Candidate.
   233	11. **Record.** The tracking record gains a `candidate` block with the worktree
   234	    path, branch, admitted branch and commit, control root, harness home,
   235	    credential bindings and final disposition (`published`, `published-retained`
   236	    or `retained`, plus
   237	    the Candidate commit when one exists). Changed paths, proof selectors,
   238	    report entries and citations are Candidate-relative. The tracking-record
   239	    citation (metadata only, per #1) and `build-summary.json`'s
   240	    `full_record.path` resolve from control. Review packets and record-version
   241	    sidecars stay under control's `.kogen/runtime/scenario-tracking/<build-id>/`
   242	    with control-relative locators, which the live fixtures' retention and
   243	    `Kogen.ReviewPacketAudit` read (scenario
   244	    `shape-to-build-retains-record-sidecars`).
   245	12. **Candidate commands, current project only.** They act only on Candidates
   246	    that have an owner record under the current control checkout's
   247	    `<project-id>`:
   248	    - `mix kogen.candidates` lists them with build id, slug, title, status,
   249	      start time, branch and path.
   250	    - `mix kogen.candidates.remove <build-id> [--discard-accepted]` deletes the
   251	      worktree (forced, since a stopped Candidate's uncommitted edits are
   252	      expected), the branch (`-D`), the harness home and the owner record.
   253	    - Remove refuses a `running` Candidate. For a Candidate holding a commit:
   254	      if the commit is reachable from its admitted branch (the Shaper
   255	      fast-forwarded it, or `published: cleanup refused`), it is removed without
   256	      a flag; if not, it is refused unless `--discard-accepted` is given. It deletes only paths under
   257	      `<workspaces-root>/<project-id>/` that match the owner record and are
   258	      registered in this repository's `git worktree list`.
   259	    - Both commands print what they removed or refused. There is no prune or
   260	      orphan cleanup (parked, `SHAPE_CANDIDATE_PRUNE.md`).
   261	
   262	13. **Role write boundary (BLD-12), enforced by macOS.** Every role process tree of a Build
   263	    runs inside one macOS Seatbelt profile, applied by the kernel at launch
   264	    (`/usr/bin/sandbox-exec -p <profile>`). That covers the fresh Developer, every resume, the
   265	    Reviewer, their native helpers, hooks (including Stop and any `make` it or the Developer
   266	    starts), `mix kogen.expert` and the Expert it launches, and anything any of them spawns.
   267	    Descendants inherit the profile and can't remove it. The mechanism was chosen by probing
   268	    (`evidence/write-boundary-probe-2026-09-25.md`). Neither harness's own permission layer
   269	    confines the harness process's own writes (the Write tool, apply_patch, session files),
   270	    and Codex's Seatbelt can't nest inside another profile.
   271	    - **One Build-wide profile.** `Kogen.Build.WriteBoundary` renders it once at admission
   272	      from canonical, symlink-resolved paths. It is `(allow default)`, then
   273	      `(deny file-write*)`, then writes allowed only under:
   274	      - the Candidate;
   275	      - the Build's harness home;
   276	      - a spaceless per-Build temp dir under the controller's canonical system temp dir,
   277	        `kogen-build-<build-id>/`. It is created at admission and removed at every Build exit,
   278	        and every launch gets it as `TMPDIR`, `TMPPREFIX` (zsh here-documents) and
   279	        `CLAUDE_CODE_TMPDIR` (Claude Code's Bash tool);
   280	      - `KOGEN_RAW_LOG_DIR` when the controller has it set (retained evidence, created by the
   281	        controller at admission if missing; it must not be or contain the control root,
   282	        control's `.git`, `.kogen/build.lock` or `.kogen/runtime/scenario-tracking/`);
   283	      - the stdio and pty devices;
   284	      - the login keychain file and its `.sb-` temp files, because Claude Code persists
   285	        rotated OAuth tokens through `/usr/bin/security` in-process;
   286	      - `<Claude scope>/.oauth_refresh.lock` when the route uses Claude Code;
   287	      - the Codex scope when the route uses Codex, minus the entries Kogen's scope validation
   288	        refuses or owns: `hooks.json`, `plugins/`, `rules/`, `config.d/`, `AGENTS.md`,
   289	        `AGENTS.override.md`, `environments.toml`, `agents/` and `.kogen-owned`.
   290	
   291	      `/bin/ps` is the one executable run outside the profile, because sandboxed processes
   292	      can't exec setuid binaries and Kogen's own code reads the process table with it.
   293	      LaunchServices opens (`lsopen`) and Apple Events (`appleevent-send`) are denied, because
   294	      they hand work to processes outside the profile. The same profile applies to every role
   295	      of the Build, so an Expert on the other harness runs inside the Developer's profile.
   296	    - **Everything else is refused, and named.** Writes to the control checkout (including
   297	      `.kogen/`, the build lock and the tracking record), control's Git metadata (the
   298	      Candidate's index, objects, refs and config live there, so role `git add`, `commit`,
   299	      `stash`, `checkout -b`, `update-ref` and `config` fail), other Candidates, other harness
   300	      homes, owner records, the Claude scope directory, the user's home and every other path
   301	      fail with `EPERM` ("Operation not permitted"). That includes writes through symlinks,
   302	      hardlinks, renames and `/tmp` aliases. Reads, network and process execution stay open.
   303	      Role `git status` and `git diff` still work.
   304	    - **The launcher.** `Kogen.Harness` Build role launches (adapters' `run_with_stdin`, the
   305	      Codex review and exec paths, Build readiness such as `claude auth status` and
   306	      `codex login status`) take the boundary from the launch context and prefix the command.
   307	      Readiness runs inside the boundary too, so a grant a real harness needs fails before any
   308	      model call. Shape, login, status outside a Build, and Shaping sessions are not wrapped.
   309	      Their containment is the Shaping half of BLD-12, owned by
   310	      `headless-shaping-and-question-inbox`. Every wrapped launch sets `KOGEN_WRITE_BOUNDARY` to
   311	      the profile sha256. Kogen's own state root (`~/Library/Application Support/Kogen/…`:
   312	      runtimes, selectors, Codex `operations/` and `active/` leases) is outside the grants. So
   313	      `mix kogen.expert` run from the Developer's tree in a Build takes no Codex lease and makes
   314	      no operation dir there. It uses the frozen binding from `KOGEN_EXPERT`, puts its
   315	      operation root under the harness home, and relies on the lease the Build controller
   316	      already holds for that harness from admission to exit (`Harness.open_roles/3`).
   317	    - **Fails closed.** Every grant is resolved to its canonical path first, because the
   318	      kernel matches only real paths and `/tmp/…` or `/var/folders/…` aliases would silently
   319	      never match (probe 14). If `/usr/bin/sandbox-exec` is missing, a grant can't be resolved
   320	      to an existing directory, a grant is `/`, `/private/tmp`, `/private/var`, `$HOME`, the control
   321	      root or one of its ancestors, or the admission self-test (the profile applied to
   322	      `/usr/bin/true`) fails, admission stops before any readiness call or launch, naming the
   323	      reason. There is no unwrapped fallback.
   324	    - **Already confined.** The kernel refuses to apply a different profile inside a sandbox
   325	      (`sandbox_apply: Operation not permitted`, probe 2). Confinement is detected by the kernel,
   326	      never by the environment: applying `(version 1)(allow default)` to `/usr/bin/true` exits 71
   327	      only inside a sandbox. If the controller is confined and `KOGEN_WRITE_BOUNDARY` is set (a
   328	      fixture Build that a Developer runs in its own shell, or `mix kogen.expert` from the
   329	      Developer's tree), role launches run without re-applying and the record says `inherited`.
   330	      They are still inside the enclosing role's boundary. If it is confined without the marker,
   331	      the Build stops naming the foreign sandbox. If it is not confined, the marker is ignored
   332	      and the profile is applied.
   333	    - **The controller stays outside.** The parent controller and its own verification children
   334	      (#4: `make check` and the selected targets, `make -C <Candidate>`) run unconfined, with
   335	      `KOGEN_WRITE_BOUNDARY` removed from their environment. The controller must write the
   336	      record, lock and receipts, and fixture Builds inside `check` and live targets must apply
   337	      their own boundary, which the kernel would refuse inside another profile.
   338	    - **Kept flags.** Claude Code keeps `--dangerously-skip-permissions`. Codex keeps
   339	      `--dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox` and `--dangerously-bypass-hook-trust`. The
   340	      enforcement is the enclosing kernel profile, which already covers everything those
   341	      harness layers would, including the harness's own writes, and Codex's own Seatbelt would
   342	      fail to nest inside it (probe 10). README says so.
   343	    - **Record.** The tracking record gains a `boundary` block: mode (`applied` or `inherited`),
   344	      the profile sha256, the grant list, the denied scope entries and the `sandbox-exec` path.
   345	
   346	## Installing this Intent (self-hosting, D9)
   347	
   348	The installing Build runs under the then-main controller (with #1 to #4), in
   349	the one checkout, not isolated. It reads several files **from disk at run
   350	time**, so the Candidate must leave them byte-identical:
   351	
   352	- `.codex/hooks.json` and `.codex/hooks/**`. `VerificationPolicy.preflight`
   353	  byte-compares the PreToolUse command before every Developer launch
   354	  (`lib/kogen/verification_policy.ex:14,59-85` at `363c20af`; #4 changes what it requires), and the inert
   355	  Stop bootstrap stays until ROADMAP order 10.
   356	- `priv/kogen/claude_code/settings.json`, passed by path to every Claude launch
   357	  (`lib/kogen/harness/claude.ex:26,164-165` at `363c20af`).
   358	- `priv/kogen/prompts/*.md`, read at each role launch.
   359	- `Makefile` and `priv/kogen/verification_targets.yaml` (#4 keeps them frozen).
   360	- `.kogen/config.yaml`.
   361	
   362	None of these are in `may_change_guarded_paths`. The installing Build itself
   363	never runs in a Candidate: then-main has no workspace admission, and Candidate
   364	code can't change the controller that runs it. The first real Candidate is
   365	created by the `live-shape-to-build` fixture Build inside this Build (running
   366	the Candidate's code), and then by the first ordinary Build after this lands. Everything this Intent adds
   367	is used only by the Candidate's own code: the Build's tests and the
   368	`live-shape-to-build` fixture Build. It is an expand step with nothing to
   369	contract later.
   370	
   371	- The inert Stop bootstrap and the `$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)`
   372	  PreToolUse command stay. Removing them is ROADMAP order 10.
   373	- The engine limitation stays. The controller of a later Build runs from
   374	  control's compiled code and reads control's `priv/`, so the Shaper editing
   375	  Kogen's own engine files in control during a Build can affect it. Pinned
   376	  generations are ROADMAP order 6.
   377	- Editing Drafts and other ignored files is safe.
   378	- The installing Build's own roles run without a write boundary, because then-main has none.
   379	  The boundary first confines the nested fixture Builds of `live-shape-to-build` and
   380	  `live-reviewer-rework` (Candidate code, launched by the controller's unconfined `make`), and
   381	  then the first ordinary Build after this lands. No `.claude/settings.json` is added to this
   382	  repository: `--setting-sources project` would load it into the installing Build's own roles.
   383	  The SessionStart probe lives only in the live fixture's copy.
   384	
   385	While this installing Build runs, **no session may write in the checkout**,
   386	because the running controller still guards the one checkout.
   387	
   388	## Outcome walkthrough and challenge
   389	
   390	1. The Shaper runs `mix kogen.build my-slug` on clean `main` at `A`.
   391	2. The Candidate is created at
   392	   `…/build-workspaces/<pid>/my-slug-Q7…/` on `kogen/my-slug/Q7…`, with the
   393	   harness home `…/<pid>/harness/Q7…/`.
   394	3. Readiness runs `claude auth status` with `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR=<home>/claude`
   395	   and `CLAUDE_SECURESTORAGE_CONFIG_DIR=<shared scope>`, and gets
   396	   `loggedIn: true`. On a hybrid route, the Codex Reviewer's login is checked
   397	   the same way.
   398	4. The Developer starts in the Candidate. Meanwhile the Shaper saves a Draft
   399	   and approves another package in control. Both are ignored paths, so control
   400	   stays clean and the Build's guard never sees them.
   401	5. The turn ends. The controller runs `make -C <Candidate> check`, which fails,
   402	   and resumes the same session in the same Candidate and config dir. It then
   403	   passes, and the controller builds the report and packet from the Candidate.
   404	6. The Reviewer (Claude Code on `default_route: claude`, or Codex on a hybrid
   405	   route), with cwd the Candidate, asks for rework. The same
   406	   Developer session resumes, verification passes, and a fresh Reviewer
   407	   accepts.
   408	7. Throughout, every role process and everything it spawns runs inside the
   409	   Build's Seatbelt profile. A Developer command `echo x > <control>/README.md`,
   410	   a helper's `git -C <control> update-ref …` or a Write-tool edit of
   411	   `~/.zshrc` fails with "Operation not permitted", while its edits in the
   412	   Candidate, its session files in the harness home and its here-documents
   413	   in the Build temp dir work. The record says `boundary: applied`.
   414	8. The controller commits in the Candidate, checks `main == A` and a clean
   415	   control, and fast-forwards `main`. Control shows the commit and a clean
   416	   status, the worktree and branch are gone, and the harness home keeps the
   417	   transcripts.
   418	
   419	Plausible implementations that pass naive checks but miss the outcome:
   420	
   421	- The worktree exists and roles `cd` there, but Candidate id, changed paths,
   422	  guards or selectors are still computed from control. Control is clean at
   423	  `A`, so the report says "no changes", and a missing selector looks present.
   424	- `make` targets still run in control (or in `File.cwd!()`), so a failing
   425	  Candidate passes against control's clean tree.
   426	- A per-Build `HOME` for Claude Code. Every role then reports "not logged in"
   427	  (probe B). Or a fresh `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` without the secure-storage
   428	  reference (probe C). Or a copied `.credentials.json` or Keychain export: a
   429	  forbidden copy that forks the refresh token.
   430	- The login scope is keyed on the Candidate path, so a project-scope login is
   431	  never found. Or the scope is re-resolved per launch, so a mid-Build selector
   432	  change switches logins inside one Build.
   433	- Inherited `CLAUDE_SECURESTORAGE_CONFIG_DIR` is left alone, so an empty value
   434	  silently uses the personal login (probe F).
   435	- Hook commands and settings are rewritten to "run from control". That
   436	  reintroduces the `cFnHwg7P` preflight failure under main's controller and
   437	  isn't needed, because the controller already judges.
   438	- Guard capture still includes control's ignored files, so a mid-Build Draft
   439	  fails the Build.
   440	- `_build/` is copied, carrying links into control.
   441	- Publication uses `update-ref` and leaves control's index stale, or treats
   442	  "no conflicts" as publishable after `main` moved.
   443	- Removal is `--force` on success.
   444	- The harness home is deleted on publication before the live audit reads the
   445	  transcripts, or it is deleted with a foreign worktree.
   446	- Offline tests keep `File.cd!(repo, fn -> Build.run(slug) end)` and assert on
   447	  `repo`'s HEAD, which passes even if Build still works in the invoking
   448	  checkout.
   449	- The boundary wraps only the fresh Developer, or is set as harness
   450	  permission flags (Claude Code `permissions.deny`, Codex `--sandbox
   451	  workspace-write`) that leave the harness process's own writes and Kogen's
   452	  bypass flags in place.
   453	- Grants use `/tmp/...` or `/var/folders/...` aliases, so the kernel never
   454	  matches them and every role fails, or the fix is a broad `/private/tmp`,
   455	  `$HOME` or control grant.
   456	- `sandbox-exec` failing (missing, or `sandbox_apply` refused) falls back to
   457	  an unwrapped launch, or an inherited `KOGEN_WRITE_BOUNDARY` alone turns the
   458	  boundary off.
   459	- The controller's `make check` runs inside a role boundary, so every
   460	  fixture Build in the suite fails with `sandbox_apply: Operation not
   461	  permitted`, or fixtures pass by special-casing `KOGEN_HARNESS`.
   462	- The Codex scope is left unwritable (the real Reviewer can't write its
   463	  rollout or refresh its login) or fully writable (a role plants
   464	  `hooks.json` or `plugins/`, lesson 3).
   465	
   466	Each scenario names the sentinel or control that rejects these.
